![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

While renaming `error` to `_error` satisfies the `no-unused-vars` lint rule, this `catch` block could be more robust. It currently assumes any error from the `Report_VatObligations` API call is due to the account not being VAT-registered, and returns a success response with an informational message. This can hide other problems like network issues or authentication failures.

A better approach is to inspect the error and only handle the specific case for non-VAT-registered accounts. Other unexpected errors should be re-thrown to be handled by the main error handler at the end of the function.

You'll need to import `QuickFileApiError` from `../api/client.js` for this suggestion to work.

```typescript
        } catch (error) {
          // The QuickFile API may not return a specific error code for non-VAT-registered accounts.
          // A common pattern is to check the error message. You may need to adjust the strings to match the actual API error.
          if (
            error instanceof QuickFileApiError &&
            (error.message.includes("not VAT registered") ||
              error.message.includes("MTD not configured"))
          ) {
            // If the account is not VAT registered or MTD not set up, return helpful message
            return successResult({
              count: 0,
              obligations: [],
              message:
                "VAT obligations not available. This account may not be VAT registered or MTD VAT may not be configured.",
            });
          }
          // For all other errors, let the generic handler deal with it.
          throw error;
        }
